Native raylib 6.0 for generated9

Build in a generated9 terminal:

    cd /sys/src/libraylib
    mk install
    cd examples
    mk install
    games/raydemo

The examples use the native compiler and linker. Client programs include
<u.h>, <libc.h>, then <raylib.h>; use the compiler's -p option for ANSI cpp.
The public header selects libraylib automatically. <raymath.h> supplies the
math helpers; <rlgl.h> exposes the compiled classic rendering interface.
Do not define RLGL_IMPLEMENTATION in clients of the installed library.
For matrix arrays, use a named float16 from MatrixToFloatV and its .v member;
the native compiler cannot decay an array member of a returned structure.

Run graphics programs inside a rio window, with /dev/gpu providing a VirGL
context (QEMU virtio-vga-gl and a host OpenGL renderer). This is the same
accelerated path used by the existing TinyGL programs. The current target
limit is 2048 by 2048 pixels. Rendering is read back and presented through
libdraw. No foreign compiler, GLFW, SDL, Mesa guest library or C++ is used.

The backend provides one resizable rio window, keyboard, Unicode text,
mouse buttons and wheel, cursor locking, /dev/snarf text and plumber URLs.
Fullscreen, window decorations and placement policy belong to rio.
Gamepads, touch devices, clipboard images, window icons, opacity and physical
monitor measurements are unavailable. Unsupported window requests log a
warning. Window flags cannot make a rio window non-resizable. Resizing and
placement require the current window; pointer warps require the pointer to
be inside it, as specified by rio(4).

The renderer uses raylib's OpenGL 1.1 path: shapes, textures, fonts, cameras
and fixed-function models. Programmable shaders, render textures, instancing
and the modern OpenGL examples require a different renderer. This port does
not claim complete OpenGL conformance; see tinygl(2) for the supported subset.

Audio uses /dev/audio (signed 16-bit little-endian, stereo, 44100 Hz). The
upstream float mixer and resampler run in a shared-memory process. The usual
sound, music stream, raw stream and callback APIs retain their roles. Release
sounds and streams before CloseAudioDevice. Callbacks run in the mixer and
must not call back into APIs which acquire the audio lock.

games/raydemo -a enables a short sound when Space toggles the animation.
-n limits the number of frames, for unattended smoke tests. Space pauses,
arrows rotate the camera, and the mouse wheel changes the viewing distance.

FileRename supports paths within the same directory; moving files between
directories is not an atomic operation provided by the native file API.

The source revision, licenses and port changes are recorded in UPSTREAM.

Validation (amd64, QEMU with VirGL and an HDA audio device):

    cd /sys/src/libraylib/test
    mk test
    ./6.out -a

The -a test requires /dev/audio. It checks generated WAV data, resampling,
playback, stream pause/resume and device recreation. The default tests check
file operations, packed component layouts, images, texture updates, screen
capture and repeated window/context creation. The example needs no assets.

Additional smoke tests used the pinned upstream example assets: OBJ cube,
IQM guy, glTF robot, M3D Suzanne, VOX fez, the PixAntiqua TrueType font, and
WAV/OGG/MP3/QOA/XM audio. These downloadable assets are not installed or
vendored. Rendering and PCM delivery were exercised; the automated VM used
QEMU's silent audio backend. Other architectures have not been tested.
